      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">
        5. Healthcare Contributions & Mandatory Social Security: GESY vs Malta NI
      </h2>
      <p>
        A frequent trap for digital nomads evaluating headline tax rates is overlooking mandatory national health insurance and social security levies:
      </p>
      <p>
        In Cyprus, the <strong>General Healthcare System (GESY)</strong> is mandatory for all tax residents. While non-doms are exempt from SDC, they remain subject to GESY contributions on both active employment and passive investment income (including dividends and rental yields). The GESY rate on dividend and interest income is <strong>2.65%</strong>. Crucially, total annual GESY contributions are subject to a strict statutory ceiling: they are levied only up to €180,000 of cumulative income. Consequently, the maximum healthcare liability you can ever incur in Cyprus is <strong>€4,770 per year</strong>, making it exceptionally attractive for high-net-worth individuals.
      </p>
      <p>
        In Malta, beneficiaries of the Nomad Residence Permit are explicitly exempt from paying Maltese National Insurance (NI) contributions, provided they maintain private comprehensive health insurance covering all risks across the Maltese islands and the Schengen territory. However, if a nomad transitions to Malta's standard tax residency or establishes a local employment contract, Maltese NI contributions apply at approximately 10% of gross salary, capped at approximately €54 per week.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">
